Down Syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Upward slanting palpebral fissures, Single transverse palmar crease, Generalized hypotonia, Trisomy 21

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-018
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress and suspected lower respiratory tract infection.
Location: Group residential home
Present at Visit: Patient and support worker
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult with Trisomy 21 who developed a rapid-onset fever, severe lethargy, and a harsh, wet cough over the last 12 hours, with an increased work of breathing. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the known immunological vulnerabilities and generalized hypotonia in Down Syndrome patients, which drastically increases the risk of rapid decompensation from bacterial pneumonia.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He is burning up, breathing very fast, and is too weak to get out of bed or drink his fluids. (Reported by support worker)
History of Present Illness: The support worker reports a 12-hour history of acute deterioration in a 22-year-old male with a known diagnosis of Trisomy 21 (Down Syndrome). The patient developed a sudden high fever, chills, and a productive cough with yellow sputum. Due to baseline generalized hypotonia, the patient has a weak cough reflex and is currently struggling to clear his secretions. The patient has mild intellectual disability but is usually verbal and interactive; currently, he is severely lethargic and withdrawn. There is a known history of an atrioventricular septal defect repaired in childhood.
Functional Status: Usually independent with ADLs in a supported environment; currently bedbound due to severe fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of rapid respiratory failure due to hypotonia and impaired secretion clearance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 38.9 C | RR: 28 bpm | SpO2: 92% (Room Air) | Wt: 65.0 Kg
General: Patient is flushed, diaphoretic, and using accessory muscles to breathe. Characteristic facial features are present (upward slanting palpebral fissures, flat facial profile).
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals tachypnea and decreased chest expansion. Auscultation reveals focal coarse crackles and bronchial breath sounds at the left lower lung base. A single transverse palmar crease is noted on both hands. Cardiac exam reveals a regular tachycardia with a healed sternotomy scar; no new murmurs. Neurological exam confirms baseline generalized hypotonia, currently worsening his ability to mount an effective cough. The tongue is protruding slightly due to lethargy and a small oral cavity.
Environmental Observations: Room is stuffy; support staff have appropriately isolated him from other residents.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine respiratory medications. Paracetamol was given 4 hours ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Down Syndrome (complicated by acute left lower lobe pneumonia) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a high fever, purulent sputum, and strictly unilateral (focal) lung crackles rather than bilateral basal crepitations.

Viral Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the high fever, focal consolidation signs on auscultation, and the degree of systemic toxicity and hypoxia (SpO2 92%).

Aspiration Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause, as there is no history of a choking event, though baseline hypotonia always poses a mild chronic risk.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Vulnerable adult requiring close monitoring)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a high fever, focal lung crackles, and hypoxia in the context of Trisomy 21 strongly indicates an acute bacterial pneumonia. The objective findings of tachypnea (RR 28) and border-line oxygen saturations, compounded by baseline hypotonia leading to a weak cough, confirmed a high risk for rapid respiratory decompensation. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because timely initiation of empirical broad-spectrum oral antibiotics and close clinical observation were mandated to prevent septic shock and to decide if immediate hospital admission was required.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced empirical oral Amoxicillin/Clavulanate 875/125mg BD. Administered Ibuprofen to reduce the fever and decrease the metabolic demand. Guided support staff in assisted coughing techniques (chest percussion and postural drainage).
Positive Behavioral Change: When you sit him completely upright in a chair to eat or drink, gravity protects his lungs; therefore, enforcing a strict upright posture protocol prevents secondary aspiration while he is weak. When you prompt him to take three deep breaths and cough forcefully every hour, you help clear the mucus; rewarding this effort with a preferred activity builds compliance with this vital chest physiotherapy.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Requested the community nursing team to visit tomorrow morning to reassess vital signs.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a bedside humidifier.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow to assess treatment response; low threshold for ED transfer if SpO2 drops further.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if his lips or fingers turn blue, if his breathing rate goes over 30 breaths per minute, or if he becomes unarousable or confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive based on clinical signs; a chest X-ray will be required when he is stable enough to travel.
Clinical Course: The fever should begin to respond to antibiotics within 48 hours. The cough will likely persist for 2-3 weeks, requiring ongoing chest physiotherapy due to his low muscle tone.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Support staff must check his temperature and breathing rate every 4 hours overnight. Seek urgent care if his oxygen saturation on the home monitor drops below 92% consistently.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The infection in his left lung is thick and hard to clear because his baseline muscle tone is low. The antibiotics will kill the bacteria, but he must be helped to cough up the mucus to truly clear the infection.
Actionable Steps: Administer the antibiotics exactly on time. Perform assisted coughing exercises hourly while awake. Check vitals every 4 hours tonight.

